WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.542 --> 00:00:05.130
Imagine a molecule that you created on a computer
capable of saving lives.

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:10.802
Instead of having to go around nature
and dig into soil and water,

00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:13.221
which is a very painstaking process,

00:00:13.221 --> 00:00:14.681
we do everything on the computer.

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:18.935
Tools like ChatGPT are helping us
accelerate scientific discovery.

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:23.231
We can now uncover
new antibiotic molecules in a few hours

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:24.816
instead of five, six years.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:34.409
Modern medicine as we know it
would collapse without effective antibiotics.

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:39.539
Antimicrobial resistance is ... you know,
I really can't think of anything more consequential.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.124
These are bacterial infections

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:45.962
that are associated with about five million
deaths per year around the world.

00:00:46.254 --> 00:00:51.468
If we don't come up with new solutions
by 2050, that number is projected to double.

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:55.764
Routine interventions, surgeries,
even childbirth,

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:57.390
you need to have antibiotics that work.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:01.019
One of the big breakthroughs
that we've made

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:04.105
is thinking of all of biology as information.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:06.357
The “code of life” is just a code.

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:08.568
That gives us this superpower

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.987
of being able explore the tree of life.

00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:13.239
We’ve explored venoms.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.784
We’ve looked at the genetic code
of woolly mammoths,

00:01:15.784 --> 00:01:18.745
of ancient penguins
that were extinct in the 50s.

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:21.456
A lot of the molecules that we've discovered,

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:25.293
we’ve seen that they are indeed capable
of killing contemporary pathogens.

00:01:25.502 --> 00:01:26.878
I could synthesize next week.

00:01:26.878 --> 00:01:27.295
Let’s go.

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:29.589
ChatGPT has really eliminated

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:33.343
that barrier for scientists to be able to do
computational biology.

00:01:33.843 --> 00:01:38.389
It can enable them to analyze data
in new ways, to write algorithms.

00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:44.062
We're trying to decipher all these secrets
that are embedded in biology.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:49.609
AI systems can run through that vast complexity
and identify patterns.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:51.736
One of the dreams that we have

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:55.657
is that one of the many molecules
that we have designed on the computer,

00:01:56.241 --> 00:02:00.537
they can save lives, right? Reduce suffering,
benefit humanity in some way.

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:04.290
It's part of the fire that we feel
every single day

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.625
to be able to help people.
